## CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION AND MOTIVATION

### 1.1 The Insufficiency of Monolithic Intelligence

The dominant paradigm in AI system design has been parametric scaling — the assumption that a single model, scaled sufficiently in parameters and training data, will converge toward general intelligence. This assumption is under severe theoretical and empirical pressure from at least three directions.

First, specialization and generalization impose conflicting gradient pressures during training. A model optimized for code generation acquires representational structure that actively interferes with emotional reasoning tasks, and vice versa. No single parameter configuration minimizes loss uniformly across a heterogeneous task distribution. Second, error accumulation in sequential reasoning is structurally different from error in single-pass generation: iterative loops amplify small hallucinations into large semantic drift, a dynamic that single-model architectures have no native mechanism to certify against. Third, thermodynamic efficiency — the ratio of useful information produced to physical energy consumed — is maximized by routing tasks to models whose learned representations already compress that task's information structure, not by routing everything through one over-parameterized model.

Multi-agent orchestration addresses all three pressures simultaneously. Drammeh et al. (2026) report substantial improvements in actionable recommendation rates comparing multi-agent orchestration to single-agent approaches across controlled trials. The gap is structural, not calibrational — it reflects the mathematical impossibility of simultaneously optimizing conflicting objectives in a single parameter space.

The central thesis of this dissertation is as follows:

> **AGI-class behavior is not a property of any single model. It is an emergent property of a formally orchestrated system of specialized agents operating on a Riemannian semantic manifold, subject to PAC-optimal routing, Riemannian contraction stability, and Landauer-grounded thermodynamic efficiency constraints.**

Formally, we define:

$$\mathcal{AGI} \;\triangleq\; \mathcal{O}\bigl(\{\mathcal{M}_i\}_{i=1}^{N},\; \mathcal{R},\; \mathcal{F},\; \mathcal{V},\; \mathcal{T},\; \mathfrak{M}\bigr)$$

where $\mathcal{O}$ is the orchestration operator, $\{\mathcal{M}_i\}$ is the model ensemble, $\mathcal{R}$ is the routing policy, $\mathcal{F}$ is the fusion operator, $\mathcal{V}$ is the validation operator, $\mathcal{T}$ is the task taxonomy, and $\mathfrak{M}$ is the Riemannian semantic manifold on which all validation and fusion dynamics operate.

