## CHAPTER 10: EMPIRICAL VERIFICATION PROTOCOLS

### 10.1 Verifying Lemma 0

Construct the empirical Gram matrix $\hat{G}$ using mean embeddings of task prototypes from a benchmark corpus (MMLU, HELM, or equivalent). Compute all off-diagonal entries $\hat{G}_{kj}$ and verify $\max_{k\neq j} |\hat{G}_{kj}| \leq 1 - \hat{\Delta}$ for some $\hat{\Delta} > 0$. Apply Gershgorin: if $(K-1)(1-\hat{\Delta}) < 1$, the empirical completeness certificate holds. Report $\hat{\varepsilon}_0 = 1 - \frac{1}{K}\sum_k \max_{x \in T_k} \langle \phi(x), b_k \rangle$ and verify $\hat{\varepsilon}_0 < 0.15$.

### 10.2 Verifying MROT-S

For each model-task pair, collect $n = 500$ performance samples on held-out benchmark tasks. Compute $\hat{\mu}_{ik}$, $\hat{\sigma}_{ik}^2$, and verify the Bernstein bound: the confidence interval $[\hat{\mu}_{ik} - \varepsilon, \hat{\mu}_{ik} + \varepsilon]$ should contain the oracle mean $\mu_{ik}$ (estimated from $n = 5000$ samples) in at least $1 - \delta = 0.95$ of trials. Report empirical $n_{\text{eff}}$ for specialist versus generalist models and verify the predicted 30–50% reduction.

### 10.3 Verifying CVF-R

On a validation corpus of 10,000 embedding pairs, compute $\hat{L}$ (empirical Lipschitz constant of $V_\mathcal{M}$) and $\hat{\mu}$ (empirical monotonicity constant). Compute $\hat{q}^* = \sqrt{1 - \hat{\mu}^2/\hat{L}^2 + r^2}$ and compare to the observed convergence rate from the slope of $\log d_g(y^{(k)}, y^{(0)})$ versus $k$. Agreement within 5% on non-adversarial tasks constitutes validation.

### 10.4 Verifying CTB-L

Measure $E^{\text{actual}}$ (GPU energy consumption per inference, in joules) and estimate $\Delta H_i(x)$ (output entropy reduction per task, in nats). Compute $\hat{\eta}_M = k_B T \ln 2 \cdot \Delta H_i(x) / E^{\text{actual}}$ and verify $\hat{\eta}_M > 0$ for all routing policies. Compare $\hat{\eta}_M$ across routing policies to confirm $\eta_M(\mathcal{R}^*) > \eta_M(\mathcal{R}_{\text{random}})$.

### 10.5 Verifying Memory ISS

Implement the DCO memory dynamics with varying $\lambda_{LT}$, $\lambda_{SG}$. Estimate $L_{g_1}$ by adversarial prompting: inject 1000 hallucination-corrupted outputs and measure $\|\Delta K_{SG}(t)\|/\|\Delta K_{LT}(0)\|$ across trials. Verify the Small-Gain condition $\lambda_{LT}\lambda_{SG} > \hat{L}_{g_1}$ and confirm that memory corruption $\|(K_{LT}(t), K_{SG}(t))\|$ remains bounded and decays under repeated hallucination injection.

