### A.6 Rate-Distortion Theory and Landauer: Complete Treatment

**Theorem A.11 (Rate-Distortion Theorem, Shannon 1959).** For a memoryless source with distribution $p(x)$ and single-letter distortion measure $d: \mathcal{X} \times \hat{\mathcal{X}} \to [0, \infty)$, the rate-distortion function:

$$R(D) = \min_{\substack{p(\hat{x}|x):\\ \mathbb{E}[d(X,\hat{X})] \leq D}} I(X;\hat{X})$$

satisfies: (i) $R(D)$ is non-increasing and convex; (ii) $R(0) = H(X)$ for a finite discrete source; (iii) $R(D) = 0$ for $D \geq D_{\max} = \min_{\hat{x}} \mathbb{E}[d(X, \hat{x})]$; (iv) for the Gaussian source with MSE distortion, $R(D) = \frac{1}{2}\log_2(\sigma^2/D)$ for $D \leq \sigma^2$.

**Theorem A.12 (Landauer's Principle, complete statement, Landauer 1961).** Any logically irreversible operation on information — specifically, any operation that reduces the number of possible states of a physical system, thereby erasing information — must be accompanied by a minimum entropy increase of $k_B \ln 2$ per bit erased in the environment (heat bath at temperature $T$), corresponding to minimum energy dissipation:

$$\Delta Q \geq k_B T \ln 2 \text{ (per bit erased)}$$

**Proof sketch (Bennett 1982 formalization).** By the second law of thermodynamics, a process that maps $2^n$ states to $2^{n-1}$ states (erasing 1 bit) reduces the information entropy of the system by $\Delta S_{\text{sys}} = k_B \ln 2$. Since total entropy cannot decrease (second law), the environment must absorb at least $\Delta S_{\text{env}} \geq k_B \ln 2$, corresponding to heat $\Delta Q = T \Delta S_{\text{env}} \geq k_B T \ln 2$.

**Lemma A.3 (Landauer Bound for LLM Inference).** An LLM generating a sequence of $L$ tokens from a vocabulary of size $V$ reduces the output entropy from $H_0 = L \log_2 V$ bits (maximum entropy over all sequences) to $H_{\text{out}}$ bits (entropy of the actual output distribution). The minimum energy dissipated is:

$$\Delta Q_{\text{inf}} \geq k_B T \ln 2 \cdot (H_0 - H_{\text{out}}) = k_B T \ln 2 \cdot \Delta H$$

This bound applies at each token generation step and accumulates across the full sequence.

